ADDITIONAL PERMISSION UNDER GNU GPL VERSION 3 SECTION 7
=======================================================

Trackt is licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 3 only
(see LICENSE). The text of the GPL itself may not be modified, so this
additional permission — which GPLv3 section 7 expressly allows a copyright
holder to grant — is recorded here instead.

The permission
--------------

As an additional permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public
License version 3, the copyright holders of Trackt give you permission to
distribute a compiled binary of the Trackt mobile client (the contents of
`apps/mobile`, linked against the rest of this repository) through an
application distribution service — including but not limited to Apple's App
Store, Google Play, and F-Droid — and to submit it to such a service, even
where that service's terms of use impose restrictions on copying,
modification, or redistribution that would otherwise conflict with sections
4 through 6 of the GNU General Public License version 3.

This permission applies only to the act of distributing the binary through
such a service. It does not restrict any right the GPL grants you: the
corresponding source of any binary distributed this way remains available
under the GNU General Public License version 3, from this repository, and
you remain free to obtain, modify, build and redistribute it yourself under
the GPL's terms.

If you propagate a modified version of this program, you may remove this
additional permission from your copy, as GPLv3 section 7 permits.

Why it exists
-------------

The GPL's terms have long been held incompatible with the App Store's usage
rules, because Apple's terms restrict how a downloaded binary may be copied
and redistributed while the GPL forbids imposing further restrictions
(GPLv3 section 10). VLC and GNU Go were both removed from the App Store on
these grounds. Android and F-Droid distribution was never affected.

Trackt's copyright holders can resolve this for the iOS build, and have
chosen to, so that self-hosters' users can install a first-party client from
the platform they are on (ADR-0008). Copyleft is otherwise unchanged: the
server, the web client and the shared packages remain GPL-3.0-only, and so
does the mobile client's source.

